DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The iikkeeccttll program controls the iked(8) daemon and provides commands to
     maintain a simple X.509 certificate authority (CA) for IKEv2 peers.

     The options are as follows:

     --qq      Don't ask for confirmation of any default options.

     --ss _s_o_c_k_e_t
             Use _s_o_c_k_e_t instead of the default _/_v_a_r_/_r_u_n_/_i_k_e_d_._s_o_c_k to communi‐
             cate with iked(8).

IIKKEEDD CCOONNTTRROOLL CCOOMMMMAANNDDSS
     The following commands are available to control iked(8):

     aaccttiivvee  Set iked(8) to active mode.

     ppaassssiivvee
             Set iked(8) to passive mode.  In passive mode no packets are sent
             to peers and no connections are initiated by iked(8).

     ccoouuppllee  Load the negotiated security associations (SAs) and flows into
             the kernel.

     ddeeccoouuppllee
             Unload the negotiated SAs and flows from the kernel.  This mode
             is only useful for testing and debugging.

     llooaadd _f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e
             Reload the configuration from the specified file.

     lloogg bbrriieeff
             Disable verbose logging.

     lloogg vveerrbboossee
             Enable verbose logging.

     mmoonniittoorr
             Monitor internal messages of the iked(8) subsystems.

     rreellooaadd  Reload the configuration from the default configuration file.

     rreesseett aallll
             Reset the running state.

     rreesseett ccaa
             Reset the X.509 CA and certificate state.

     rreesseett ppoolliiccyy
             Flush the configured policies.

     rreesseett ssaa
             Flush the running SAs.

     rreesseett uusseerr
             Flush the local user database.

     rreesseett iidd _i_k_e_i_d
             Delete all IKE SAs with matching ID.

     sshhooww ssaa
             Show internal state of active IKE SAs, Child SAs and IPsec flows.

PPKKII AANNDD CCEERRTTIIFFIICCAATTEE AAUUTTHHOORRIITTYY CCOOMMMMAANNDDSS
     In order to use public key based authentication with IKEv2, a public key
     infrastructure (PKI) has to be set up to create and sign the peer cer‐
     tificates.  iikkeeccttll includes commands to simplify maintenance of the PKI
     and to set up a simple certificate authority (CA) for iked(8) and its
     peers.

     The following commands are available to control the CA:

     ccaa _n_a_m_e ccrreeaattee [ppaasssswwoorrdd _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d]
             Create a new certificate authority with the specified _n_a_m_e.  The
             command will prompt for a CA password unless it is specified with
             the optional _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d argument.  The password will be saved in a
             protected file _i_k_e_c_a_._p_a_s_s_w_d in the CA directory and used for sub‐
             sequent commands.

     ccaa _n_a_m_e ddeelleettee
             Delete the certificate authority with the specified _n_a_m_e.

     ccaa _n_a_m_e eexxppoorrtt [ppeeeerr _p_e_e_r] [ppaasssswwoorrdd _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d]
             Export the certificate authority with the specified _n_a_m_e into the
             current directory for transport to other systems.  This command
             will create a compressed tarball called _c_a_._t_g_z in the local di‐
             rectory and optionally _c_a_._z_i_p if the ‘zip’ tool is installed.
             The optional _p_e_e_r argument can be used to specify the address or
             FQDN of the local gateway which will be written into a text file
             _p_e_e_r_._t_x_t and included in the archives.

     ccaa _n_a_m_e iinnssttaallll [_p_a_t_h]
             Install the certificate and Certificate Revocation List (CRL) for
             CA _n_a_m_e as the currently active CA or into the specified _p_a_t_h.

     ccaa _n_a_m_e cceerrttiiffiiccaattee _h_o_s_t ccrreeaattee [sseerrvveerr | cclliieenntt | ooccsspp]
             Create a private key and certificate for _h_o_s_t and sign then with
             the key of certificate authority with the specified _n_a_m_e.

             The certificate will be valid for client and server authentica‐
             tion by default by setting both flags as the extended key usage
             in the certificate; this can be restricted using the optional
             sseerrvveerr or cclliieenntt argument.  If the ooccsspp argument is specified,
             the extended key usage will be set for OCSP signing.

     ccaa _n_a_m_e cceerrttiiffiiccaattee _h_o_s_t ddeelleettee
             Deletes the private key and certificates associated with _h_o_s_t.

     ccaa _n_a_m_e cceerrttiiffiiccaattee _h_o_s_t eexxppoorrtt [ppeeeerr _p_e_e_r] [ppaasssswwoorrdd _p_a_s_s_w_o_r_d]
             Export key files for _h_o_s_t of the certificate authority with the
             specified _n_a_m_e into the current directory for transport to other
             systems.  This command will create a compressed tarball _h_o_s_t_._t_g_z
             in the local directory and optionally _h_o_s_t_._z_i_p if the ‘zip’ tool
             is installed.  The optional _p_e_e_r argument can be used to specify
             the address or FQDN of the local gateway which will be written
             into a text file _p_e_e_r_._t_x_t and included in the archives.

     ccaa _n_a_m_e cceerrttiiffiiccaattee _h_o_s_t iinnssttaallll [_p_a_t_h]
             Install the private and public key for _h_o_s_t into the active con‐
             figuration or specified _p_a_t_h.

     ccaa _n_a_m_e cceerrttiiffiiccaattee _h_o_s_t rreevvookkee
             Revoke the certificate specified by _h_o_s_t and generate a new Cer‐
             tificate Revocation List (CRL).

     sshhooww ccaa _n_a_m_e cceerrttiiffiiccaatteess [_h_o_s_t]
             Display a listing of certificates associated with CA _n_a_m_e or dis‐
